Now schema.sql. Use H2-compatible + MySQL-compatible DDL. Note: `server.error.include-message: never` — my error handler returns its own body via @RestControllerAdvice, so that's fine.

schema.sql:

```sql
CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS plot (...);
```

H2 MySQL mode supports AUTO_INCREMENT, COMMENT in column def, COMMENT ON TABLE.

data.sql: seed plots and transplants. Use fixed ids? Use INSERT with explicit columns; for AUTO_INCREMENT, let ids generate. For transplants referencing plot, need plot id — use fixed ids in INSERT for plots (id 1,2,3) and transplants reference them. With AUTO_INCREMENT, explicit id insert is allowed in H2 and MySQL.

Seed:
- plot 1 PLOT-OPEN ACTIVE (empty)
- plot 2 PLOT-FULL ACTIVE with 12 transplants (11 ALIVE, 1 PENDING? For local demo "一株死亡后可登记新植株" — reviewer can mark one dead then add). Let me make PLOT-FULL 12 plants: 10 ALIVE, 1 DEAD? No — dead releases capacity, then it wouldn't be full. Make 12 active: 11 ALIVE + 1 PENDING. Then PLOT-FULL demonstrates 13th rejection and seal rejection (pending exists).
- plot 3 PLOT-REVIEW ACTIVE with 2 plants (1 ALIVE, 1 PENDING) for review demo.

data.sql inserts must be idempotent-ish? mode always runs on each startup; in-memory DB fresh each time, fine. For tests, each test class uses own DB name, schema/data run per context — CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS avoids errors if same JVM reuses... different DB names so no clash. But INSERT in data.sql would fail on duplicate if context recreated with same DB name and DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1. Each test class gets unique DB name, and Spring context caching: two test classes with different properties → different contexts → separate DBs. OK.

